<!-- <html>/<body> are ignored - only <body>'s children are copied into the page. -->
<!--
    Gram navigation bar loader for the Oxygen WebHelp Responsive template
    (issue #179).

    Loading gram-nav.js moves the page's <p class="gram-nav"> - the per-gram
    jump links generate_dita.py emits - into <nav class="wh_tools">, the toolbar
    that already carries the maximise, collapse and print buttons, and stamps
    the marker classes resources/gram-nav.css styles. It no-ops on any page with
    no gram-nav paragraph (welcome, security, week and publication indexes).

    Bind this fragment to "webhelp.fragment.after.body.topic.page" - the END of
    <body> on TOPIC pages. Both halves of that matter:

      - end of <body>, so the toolbar and the paragraph have both been parsed
        by the time the script runs. Hence no defer/async: the move then
        happens before the first paint, with no flash of the links in their
        original position at the foot of the article. (The dark-mode fragment
        beside this one is also undeferred, for the same pre-paint reason; the
        GramFrame fragment IS deferred, because it upgrades the DOM after load.)

      - topic pages only, because gram pages are the only pages that carry the
        paragraph, and because every other placeholder that would reach the
        toolbar is already spent. Oxygen binds ONE fragment per placeholder,
        and this template has claimed webhelp.fragment.head (dark-mode),
        webhelp.fragment.head.topic.page (GramFrame), webhelp.fragment.
        before.body and .after.body (protection) and webhelp.fragment.
        after.header (search flag). See pptx-transform.opt.

    The script ships beside this fragment under resources/gram-nav.js; Oxygen's
    custom-resources fileset copies resources/** into the output at
    ${oxygen-webhelp-assets-dir}/template/resources/, so the src below mirrors
    the path convention the template's other custom scripts already use.

    The <html>/<body> wrapper is not decoration: Oxygen's includeCustomHTMLContent
    copies only <body>'s children when it finds that wrapper, and copies the WHOLE
    document - these comments included - when it does not. The two <head>-bound
    fragments beside this one use a <head> wrapper for the same reason.
-->
<html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
    <body>
        <script
            src="${oxygen-webhelp-assets-dir}/template/resources/gram-nav.js?buildId=${oxygen-webhelp-build-number}"></script>
    </body>
</html>
